Spring Transaction — Interview Cheat Sheet
PROPAGATION (7 types)
Type	One-liner	Real use
REQUIRED (default)	Join if exists, else create new	Normal business ops (order + stock)
REQUIRES_NEW	Always suspend old, start new (independent)	Audit log that must survive failure
NESTED	Savepoint inside same tx	Batch — one item fails, rest continue
SUPPORTS	Join if exists, else run without tx	Shared read method (used both ways)
NOT_SUPPORTED	Always suspend tx, run without it	Slow file/report write — avoid holding lock
MANDATORY	Must have existing tx, else throw	Method that should never run standalone
NEVER	Must NOT have tx, else throw	Health check / JMS listener

Memory trick (say out loud):

REQUIRED → "join or new"
REQUIRES_NEW → "always new"
NESTED → "savepoint"
SUPPORTS → "flexible"
NOT_SUPPORTED → "always none"
MANDATORY → "must have"
NEVER → "must not have"

One golden line for interview:

"Propagation defines how a transactional method behaves when called from within an existing transaction — whether it joins, creates new, nests, suspends, or requires/forbids one."

ISOLATION (4 levels)
Level	Prevents	Real use
READ_UNCOMMITTED	nothing	rarely used, rough analytics
READ_COMMITTED (Postgres/Oracle default)	dirty read	most business apps
REPEATABLE_READ (MySQL default)	dirty + non-repeatable read	financial statements
SERIALIZABLE	dirty + non-repeatable + phantom	seat booking, last-stock item

3 problems to know:

Dirty Read — reading uncommitted data
Non-Repeatable Read — same row, different value on 2nd read
Phantom Read — same query, different row count on 2nd read

One golden line for interview:

"Isolation defines how much one transaction can see of another concurrently running transaction's changes — trade-off between consistency and performance."

Common interview follow-ups (be ready)
Why doesn't @Transactional work on self-invocation? → Spring uses proxies (AOP); calling a method from within the same class bypasses the proxy.
Does @Transactional roll back on checked exceptions? → No, only unchecked (RuntimeException/Error) by default. Use rollbackFor = Exception.class to override.
REQUIRES_NEW vs NESTED? → REQUIRES_NEW = separate connection/commit, fully independent. NESTED = same connection, savepoint, rolled back together with outer if outer fails.
Default isolation in Spring? → DEFAULT — uses whatever the underlying DB's default is (not a fixed Spring value).
